{#- Atlas — the page shell every surface extends, and the shared chip macro.

  The generator holds no project content (decision 39): every name on a rendered page arrives as
  data. A layout that hard-codes one is a fork of the generator waiting to happen, and a test
  asserts that none of them do.

  Data this layout expects:
    project  — the project's display name, from atlas.config.json
    repo     — "owner/name", from atlas.config.json
    title    — this page's own title

  Blocks: `content` (the page), `bodyClass` (surface-specific body classes).

  The stylesheet is served at /tokens.css; the build copies theme/tokens.css there.

  Macro `chip(kind, value)` renders one status, stage or triage chip. It always emits a text
  label: colour alone is not a status, both because a chip has to survive a monochrome screen and
  colour-blind eyes, and because a label is the only part of a chip a test without a browser can
  read. Both vocabularies here are closed by the manifest schema.
-#}
{%- macro chipLabel(value) -%}
{%- set LABELS = {
  "done": "Done",
  "next": "Next",
  "parked": "Parked",
  "unplanned": "Unplanned",
  "not-started": "Not started",
  "designing": "Designing",
  "planned": "Planned",
  "development": "Development",
  "staging": "Staging",
  "release": "Release",
  "awaiting-decision": "Waiting on you",
  "moving": "Moving",
  "blocked": "Blocked"
} -%}
{{- LABELS[value] if LABELS[value] else value -}}
{%- endmacro -%}
{%- macro chip(kind, value) -%}
<span class="chip chip-{{ value }}" data-{{ kind }}="{{ value }}">{{ chipLabel(value) }}</span>
{%- endmacro -%}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{{ title }} · {{ project }}</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/tokens.css">
<script>
/* Stamps an explicit theme choice before first paint. No choice stamps nothing at all, which is
 * the default state the stylesheet's bare :root exists to serve. */
(function () {
  try {
    var choice = window.localStorage.getItem('atlas-theme');
    if (choice === 'dark' || choice === 'light') {
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', choice);
    }
  } catch (err) { /* storage unavailable: the system setting decides, which is a fine answer */ }
})();
</script>
</head>
<body class="{% block bodyClass %}page{% endblock %}">
<a class="skip-link" href="#main">Skip to the content</a>
<header class="site-head">
<p class="site-title">{{ project }}</p>
{#- Decision 22's three surfaces. Two of the three names moved in #780, and neither move was
    cosmetic:

      * "Records" became "Library".
      * "Triage" is gone as a name on the desk, because triage on the desk is now a MODAL opened
        from a feature's own header. What stays at `/mobile/` is decision 27's phone surface, and
        it keeps its own name — the one its own heading already uses. That NARROWS decision 22
        rather than overturning it: planning at a desk and glancing on a phone are still different
        activities, and removing the phone's surface on a reading nobody confirmed would be the
        expensive mistake here. -#}
<nav class="site-nav" aria-label="Surfaces">
<a href="/">Feature planning</a>
<a href="/mobile/">What needs you</a>
<a href="/library/">Library</a>
{#- M9 follow-up: `hasBacklog` rides in every page's own data (src/build.mjs's `shell`), not just
    the backlog page's own — this link has to render on every page including ones that never touch
    `proposedDesigns` directly. Absent when the page itself would be (nothing proposed to list),
    the same "no link to an empty page" rule Library/Registers already follow. -#}
{%- if hasBacklog %}
<a href="/backlog/">Feature Backlog</a>
{%- endif %}
{#- Static Web Apps' own `/.auth/logout` route (decision 7) already works with no code of Atlas's
    own — it was just never linked. `post_logout_redirect_uri` sends a signed-out visitor back to
    the site root rather than SWA's bare default logout page. -#}
<a href="/.auth/logout?post_logout_redirect_uri=/">Log out</a>
</nav>
{#- Decision 61: stop waiting on CI to notice a change already landed, from wherever you're
    looking — every page carries this, not only the feature planning surface, which is why it is
    wired here rather than through `bodyScripts` below. The `author` gate is entirely server-side
    (`POST /api/refresh` itself refuses a reader with a named reason); this button does not hide
    itself from one, the same posture `approve.js` already takes. -#}
<div class="refresh-trigger" data-refresh-trigger>
<button type="button" class="refresh-trigger-button" data-refresh-button>Refresh</button>
<p class="refresh-trigger-status" data-refresh-trigger-status role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
</div>
{#- A plain list rather than a single cycling button: the owner tried the cycling control and did
    not know what it meant without reading the label after clicking. Three named choices, one
    marked current, is legible without having to act on it first. -#}
<ul class="theme-list" aria-label="Theme" data-theme-list>
<li><button type="button" data-theme-option="system">System</button></li>
<li><button type="button" data-theme-option="light">Light</button></li>
<li><button type="button" data-theme-option="dark">Dark</button></li>
</ul>
</header>
<main class="site-main" id="main">
{% block content %}{% endblock %}
</main>
{#- The shared action modal (theme/action-modal.js) every write-back trigger opens while its
    request is in flight — approve, the three deploy stages, and refresh. Hidden by default; JS
    toggles `hidden` and `aria-hidden` together rather than relying on either alone, so a reader
    with CSS disabled and a reader using only the accessibility tree agree on whether this is
    showing. Not loaded as its own <script> — theme/refresh.js (every page) and
    theme/approve.js/theme/deploy.js (feature planning) each import it directly. -#}
<div class="action-modal-backdrop" data-action-modal-backdrop hidden>
<div class="action-modal" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="action-modal-title" aria-hidden="true" data-action-modal>
<button type="button" class="action-modal-close" data-action-modal-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
<p class="action-modal-title" id="action-modal-title" data-action-modal-title></p>
<div class="action-modal-track" data-action-modal-track>
<div class="action-modal-fill"></div>
<div class="action-modal-dot"></div>
</div>
<p class="action-modal-message" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-action-modal-message></p>
</div>
</div>
<footer class="site-foot">
<p>Built from the repository and its issues, never hand-maintained. Where this page and a record disagree, the record is right. <a href="https://github.com/{{ repo }}">{{ repo }}</a></p>
</footer>
<script>
/* Three named choices rather than one cycling button (the owner tried the cycling control and
 * could not tell what it meant without reading the label after clicking). "System" removes the
 * attribute rather than guessing a value, so the page falls back to prefers-color-scheme instead
 * of freezing whichever it happened to show. */
(function () {
  var list = document.querySelector('[data-theme-list]');
  if (!list) { return; }
  var buttons = list.querySelectorAll('[data-theme-option]');

  function read() {
    try {
      var stored = window.localStorage.getItem('atlas-theme');
      return stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark' ? stored : 'system';
    } catch (err) { return 'system'; }
  }

  function apply(choice) {
    if (choice === 'system') {
      document.documentElement.removeAttribute('data-theme');
    } else {
      document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', choice);
    }
    buttons.forEach(function (button) {
      var current = button.getAttribute('data-theme-option') === choice;
      button.setAttribute('aria-pressed', current ? 'true' : 'false');
    });
    try {
      if (choice === 'system') { window.localStorage.removeItem('atlas-theme'); }
      else { window.localStorage.setItem('atlas-theme', choice); }
    } catch (err) { /* nothing to persist to; the choice still holds for this page */ }
  }

  buttons.forEach(function (button) {
    button.addEventListener('click', function () {
      apply(button.getAttribute('data-theme-option'));
    });
  });
  apply(read());
})();
</script>
<script type="module" src="/refresh.js"></script>
{#- A surface that needs behaviour of its own beyond the refresh button above loads it here, after
    the markup it acts on. Only the feature planning surface uses this block; every other page's
    only interactivity is the theme toggle and the refresh button, both already wired above
    (decision 12: no framework runtime — this stays one small script per concern, not a bundle). -#}
{% block bodyScripts %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
